[00:00.00]# Korea's Pali-Pali Culture

[00:00.00]There is one special culture in Korea.
[00:00.00]That is the 'Pali-Pali culture'.
[00:00.00]'Pali-Pali' means doing everything very quickly.
[00:00.00]Korean people like doing everything quickly and efficiently.
[00:00.00]This culture is deeply rooted in the daily lives of Korean people.

[00:00.00]When did the Pali-Pali culture begin?
[00:00.00]Korea suffered a great war in the past.
[00:00.00]After the war ended, the country was very poor, and many things were destroyed.
[00:00.00]People had to work very hard to rebuild the country.
[00:00.00]To escape poverty, everything had to be developed quickly.
[00:00.00]During this period, Korean people worked quickly without a moment's rest.
[00:00.00]As a result, Korea's economy developed greatly in a short time.
[00:00.00]People call this the 'Miracle on the Han River'.
[00:00.00]From this time on, the Pali-Pali culture took root in Korean society.

[00:00.00]The thing that best shows Korea's Pali-Pali culture is delivery.
[00:00.00]In Korea, when you order food, it arrives very quickly.
[00:00.00]Delivery drivers riding motorcycles deliver the food quickly before it gets cold.
[00:00.00]You can receive food not only at home but also in parks or at the Han River.
[00:00.00]Even at restaurants, if you order food, it comes out in a few minutes.
[00:00.00]Korean people also eat very quickly when they eat at restaurants.
[00:00.00]When they finish eating, they don't stay long at the restaurant and get up right away.

[00:00.00]Technology and the internet were also heavily influenced by the Pali-Pali culture.
[00:00.00]Korea's internet speed is among the fastest in the world.
[00:00.00]People cannot tolerate slow internet.
[00:00.00]When a new smartphone or computer comes out, people want to buy it quickly.
[00:00.00]Public transportation like trains and subways is also very fast and accurate.
[00:00.00]Especially, if you take the high-speed train called KTX, you can go from one end of Korea to the other in a few hours.

[00:00.00]You can also see interesting aspects of the Pali-Pali culture in the daily lives of Korean people.
[00:00.00]When people take an elevator, they do not wait until the doors close.
[00:00.00]As soon as they get in the elevator, they keep pressing the 'close' button many times.
[00:00.00]Even when getting coffee from a vending machine, they put their hand in the cup holder and wait before the coffee comes out completely.
[00:00.00]When taking a bus or subway, they stand in front of the door in advance and wait before the doors open.
[00:00.00]Even in the restroom, there are people who flush the water first before finishing their business.
[00:00.00]These behaviors look very fascinating to foreigners.

[00:00.00]This Pali-Pali culture brought many advantages to Korea.
[00:00.00]Korea became a very convenient and good country to live in.
[00:00.00]Because everything can be done quickly and easily, people's lives became comfortable.
[00:00.00]Even at companies, productivity is high because work is processed quickly.

[00:00.00]However, the Pali-Pali culture also has disadvantages.
[00:00.00]Because they have to do everything quickly, people get a lot of stress.
[00:00.00]Because they always have to move busily, they lack peace of mind.
[00:00.00]Also, sometimes they make mistakes or get into accidents while hurrying work too much.
[00:00.00]There were also cases where problems arose because buildings were built too quickly.

[00:00.00]These days, there are also people trying to change this Pali-Pali culture.
[00:00.00]The number of people interested in 'living slowly' is increasing.
[00:00.00]On weekends, they enjoy leaving the city and resting in the quiet countryside.
[00:00.00]This is because working quickly is important, but health and rest are also important.
[00:00.00]In the future, I hope it becomes a society where 'fast' and 'slow' achieve harmony.
[00:00.00]Korea's Pali-Pali culture is an important part of Korean history and its current state.
